            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"A forensic declaration by God acquitting a sinner and granting right standing, typically understood in the primary Protestant view to involve the imputation of Christ's righteousness, with other traditions offering transformative, demonstrative, or covenantal views.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"A judicial or demonstrative act of declaring a person, deed, or cause to be right against a charge; in salvation, God justifies sinners specifically through forgiveness in Christ rather than by imputing a separate righteousness.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"God's act of setting a sinner right before Him, primarily through a forensic declaration of righteousness.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"To judge, declare, or demonstrate someone or something to be right against a charge.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_BROADENS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book elevates the general usage (vindicating words, deeds, or causes) into the primary definition, covering instances where God or wisdom is 'justified.' Soteriologically, it rejects the prevailing Protestant addition of 'imputed righteousness,' equating justification of the sinner strictly with the forgiveness of sins.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The book explicitly rejects the 'commonly taught' view that justification involves the imputation of Christ's active righteousness to the believer, arguing instead that God justifies the ungodly through forgiveness.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"Both agree on the declarative/judicial nature of justification, but the book broadens the core definition to encompass all biblical contexts (including non-soteriological vindication) while refining the soteriological application by stripping away the theological mechanic of imputed righteousness in favor of simple forgiveness.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"In Protestant theology, Paul's arguments in Romans 5 (where many are 'made righteous') and 2 Corinthians 5 ('become the righteousness of God') are widely seen as establishing a positive standing of righteousness that goes beyond the mere removal of guilt (forgiveness).\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
